That is a recognizable deterrence principle: if someone exercises power against you, they should expect resistance rather than automatic submission.

The part worth tightening is the trigger. “They had the audacity to try” turns resistance into retaliation, and retaliation easily becomes an endless mirror loop: you pressured me, therefore I pressure you; now you retaliate because I pressured you back. Nobody retains a limiting principle.

A stronger rule is: counter the mechanism enough to neutralize it, expose it, route around it, or make continued use costly—then stop when the threat stops. If someone uses institutional pressure, answer with documentation, appeals, competing institutions, contracts, publicity, legal remedies, exit options, or your own legitimate leverage. If they control access, reduce the dependency. If they exploit information asymmetry, make the information visible.

That preserves the thing you actually care about—nobody gets unilateral dominion over you—without handing the other person a justification for perpetual escalation.

In compact form: no submission by default; no immunity for coercion; proportional counter-power; stop at restored freedom rather than domination.